From 6bf52a636a5536ccf854feddbd3bf697f667d333 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian MacDonald <ian@netstatz.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 18:41:37 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 41/88] fix(beacon): bind the real subnet broadcast and add
 beacon_listen_interfaces

The listener bound NetworkInterfaceInfo::broadcastAddress, which came from
ifa_broadaddr. When an address has no configured brd (OpenWrt/prplOS LAN
bridges), glibc and musl report the unicast address there, so the listener
bound e.g. 192.168.60.1:13305 and never heard beacons sent to
192.168.60.255, while the kernel still treats ip|~mask as that subnet's
broadcast. The broadcast is now derived from the netmask on POSIX, as the
Windows path already did; /31 and /32 fall back to 255.255.255.255. This
also fixes the sender, which was beaconing to its own unicast address on
such hosts.

Per-interface broadcast binds are kept rather than one INADDR_ANY socket:
with SO_REUSEADDR, Linux hands each 127.0.0.1 unicast beacon only to the
most recently bound wildcard socket, so a wildcard bind in lemond would take
local discovery beacons from `lemonade scan` and the desktop app whenever it
bound after them (measured on ai4). Broadcast datagrams reach every
matching socket, so scan keeps working alongside the listener.

New config key beacon_listen_interfaces (default []: every RFC1918
interface, the previous behaviour) restricts the listener to named
interfaces, e.g. ["br-lan"] on a double-NAT gateway. IP_PKTINFO gives the
arrival interface, so a 255.255.255.255 beacon arriving on an unlisted
interface is dropped and counted in stats.wrong_interface; without
IP_PKTINFO the limited-broadcast socket is not trusted when an allow-list
is set. Changing the key rebinds without restarting the listener. /health
beacon_listener gains interfaces[] and interface_allowlist.

